{
 "esm": "1.0.0",
 "metadata": {
  "name": "ISRM_gdf_polygon",
  "description": "The InMAP ISRM over a caller-supplied POLYGON emission layer, read from an uploaded shapefile with one column per pollutant in SI units and allocated to the ISRM source cells by the fraction of each polygon's area that lies in the cell. All five pathways, lifted off isrm_point.esm. Generated from isrm_polygon.esm by data/make_gdf_documents.py.",
  "authors": [
   "EarthSciML"
  ],
  "license": "MIT",
  "created": "2026-08-21T00:00:00Z",
  "tags": [
   "isrm",
   "inmap",
   "air_quality",
   "health",
   "source_receptor",
   "polygon",
   "shapefile",
   "geodataframe"
  ],
  "references": [
   {
    "url": "https://inmap.run/blog/2019/04/20/sr/",
    "notes": "The InMAP source-receptor tutorial isrm_point.esm reproduces. This document reuses its grid, its SR matrix, its unit conversion and its health function over a different emission geometry; it has no published total of its own to match, because the emission layer it reads is an EXAMPLE this repository builds (data/make_polygon_layer.py), not an inventory anyone has published a result for."
   },
   {
    "url": "https://www2.census.gov/geo/tiger/GENZ2020/shp/cb_2020_us_county_20m.zip",
    "notes": "US Census cartographic boundary counties, 1:20,000,000, 2020 vintage - the polygons data/make_polygon_layer.py filters to one state and attaches an emission column to."
   }
  ],
  "x_esd": {
   "emission_column": "The layer's EMIS column is DECLARED, not measured: data/make_polygon_layer.py sets it to `rate * ALAND / 1e6` short tons per year - a uniform per-square-kilometre area source at a rate the script's --rate names (1.0 by default). It is read here as PRIMARY PM2.5, the pathway a per-area surrogate is most defensible for and the only one a single undifferentiated tonnage can honestly feed: splitting one number across five pathways would be inventing a speciation profile. The shipped layer (Illinois, 102 counties, 143778.461053 short tons/yr over 143778 km2) is an EXAMPLE that exercises the geometry. A real area inventory carries one column per pollutant, and extending this document to five pathways is five more parameters and five more contractions, exactly as isrm_point.esm has them.",
   "geometry_pipeline": "The shapefile reader delivers `geometry` as [index, vertex, xy] in the file's own CRS - here NAD83 geographic (EPSG:4269), the same lon/lat datum the FF10 point inventory uses - right-padded to N_PVERT vertices by repeating each ring's final vertex, which the geometry kernels evaluate as the deduplicated ring (esm-spec 8.6.1). Reprojection is the DOCUMENT's job and never the reader's, so poly_x / poly_y apply the same lcc_forward_x / lcc_forward_y templates isrm_point.esm projects its stack coordinates with. poly_ring is those two columns woven back into [record, vertex, xy]; poly_xmin/ymin/xmax/ymax are its own extremes, taken AFTER projection because the Lambert image of a lon/lat box is not a box and the bounding box of the projected ring is not the projection of the file's stored bounding box.",
   "report": {
    "description": "What a run reports, in the document's own vocabulary, so a caller drives any of these documents without a table of any of them. `pathways` lists one entry per SR array this document contracts with; `total_pm25` and `deaths` name the summed observeds; `record_field` names an observed over the record axis, whose length IS the number of emission records the loader discovered.",
    "pathways": [
     {
      "sr_array": "PrimaryPM25",
      "emissions": [
       "E_PM25"
      ],
      "concentration": "conc_PrimaryPM25"
     },
     {
      "sr_array": "SOA",
      "emissions": [
       "E_VOC"
      ],
      "concentration": "conc_SOA"
     },
     {
      "sr_array": "pNO3",
      "emissions": [
       "E_NOx"
      ],
      "concentration": "conc_pNO3"
     },
     {
      "sr_array": "pNH4",
      "emissions": [
       "E_NH3"
      ],
      "concentration": "conc_pNH4"
     },
     {
      "sr_array": "pSO4",
      "emissions": [
       "E_SOx"
      ],
      "concentration": "conc_pSO4"
     }
    ],
    "total_pm25": "TotalPM25",
    "deaths": {
     "krewski": "deathsK",
     "lepeule": "deathsL"
    },
    "record_field": "poly_area",
    "record_field_description": "An observed over the RECORD axis, whose length IS the number of emission records the loader discovered. `extent` binds that count to N_REC inside the engine, but no binding exposes the resolved metaparameter on a prepared model, so a caller that must report it reads the length of this field."
   },
   "vertex_tolerance": "A MEASURED, NAMED APPROXIMATION. Before either geometry kernel clips, it deduplicates each operand ring's consecutive vertices under the shared point-equality tolerance esm-spec 8.6.1 pins - numpy's allclose defaults, atol 1e-8 and rtol 1e-5 - which is what makes the reader's repeat-padding free. That tolerance is RELATIVE, and the spec states the operands in lon/lat, where a coordinate is order 100. These rings are in PROJECTED METRES, where a coordinate is order 1e6 over Illinois, so rtol 1e-5 is about 6 METRES and two genuinely distinct vertices closer than that are merged. Measured on the shipped layer: 3 of 1279 vertices are merged, in 3 of 102 rings, all on edges shorter than 4 m against a median edge of 2.4 km. The consequence is that a clipped share is computed against a very slightly simplified ring while poly_area - a shoelace over the ring as stored - is not, so the per-cell shares no longer sum to exactly 1: sum(E_PM25) comes out 6.1e-7 BELOW sum(poly_emis), and 4 of 2053 emitting cells differ from an independent GEOS computation by more than 1e-8 relative (worst 1.0e-4, on a cell holding 0.3% of the total; the next-worst cell is at 8e-9, which is ordinary float difference between two clipping algorithms rather than a merged vertex). It is recorded here rather than worked around because every workaround is worse: clipping in lon/lat makes the InMAP cells non-rectangular AND makes the tolerance coarser still, and translating the frame to shrink the coordinates would put a data-dependent magic offset in a physics document. The real fix belongs in the kernels - a tolerance relative to the RING's own extent rather than to the magnitude of its coordinates - and is recorded in BUGS.md.",
   "one_row_per_part": "The reader emits one row per shapefile PART, replicating a record's .dbf attributes across its parts. A multi-part county - a mainland plus its islands - therefore appears as several rows that EACH carry the record's full EMIS, which would multiply that county's emissions by its part count. The shipped Illinois layer is single-part throughout (n_parts == 1 for all 102 records), so the question does not arise here; a layer where it does needs its emission divided among its parts before the file is written, which is the layer builder's job and not this document's.",
   "generated_by": "data/make_gdf_documents.py — DO NOT EDIT BY HAND. Regenerate after changing isrm_point.esm, isrm_polygon.esm or isrm_line.esm.",
   "column_convention": {
    "emissions": {
     "PM25": "kg/yr",
     "VOC": "kg/yr",
     "NOx": "kg/yr",
     "NH3": "kg/yr",
     "SOx": "kg/yr"
    },
    "stack": {
     "STKHGT": "m",
     "STKDIAM": "m",
     "STKTEMP": "K",
     "STKVEL": "m/s"
    },
    "note": "SI throughout, because the caller hands over a GeoDataFrame rather than a published inventory in the inventory's own units. An emission column the frame omits is pruned from the document together with its pathway."
   }
  }
 },
 "metaparameters": {
  "N_SRC": {
   "type": "integer",
   "default": 52411,
   "description": "ISRM source cells - the same grid isrm_point.esm emits into."
  },
  "N_RCV": {
   "type": "integer",
   "default": 52411,
   "description": "ISRM receptor cells (== the source grid, same ordered cell set)."
  },
  "N_ALL": {
   "type": "integer",
   "default": 596444,
   "description": "Every cell of the full InMAP grid: 8 ground layers of N_SRC cells plus 19 coarse layers of 9324, laid out layer-major. The length of W/S/E/N/TotalPop/MortalityRate as the zarr stores them. This document reads only the layer-0 prefix, but it reads the arrays at their real length and slices with a declared `select`, the way isrm_point.esm does."
  },
  "N_PVERT": {
   "type": "integer",
   "default": 56,
   "description": "Vertices per emission polygon: the DECLARED width of the padded ring array, and the reader's `nvert_max`. Every ring is right-padded to this many vertices by repeating its last one, and the geometry kernels deduplicate before clipping, so the padding changes no area. It is a declaration and not a discovery: the reader raises rather than silently widening if the layer's longest ring exceeds it, which is what makes a re-built layer fail loudly instead of quietly changing shape. 56 is the longest ring in the shipped Illinois layer; rebuild data/make_polygon_layer.py for another state and this is the number to change."
  },
  "N_PSEG": {
   "type": "integer",
   "default": 55,
   "description": "Segments of an emission polygon's ring: N_PVERT - 1. The shoelace sum that gives poly_area walks segment v from vertex v to vertex v+1, so its range must stop one short of the vertex axis or v+1 leaves the array. It loses nothing: a shapefile ring is explicitly closed and repeat-padded, so the segment the range omits joins the ring's last stored vertex to its first, and both are the same point. Kept as its own metaparameter rather than an arithmetic expression because an index set's size is a name or a literal, not an expression - if N_PVERT changes, this changes with it."
  },
  "N_CVERT": {
   "type": "integer",
   "default": 4,
   "description": "Vertices per SOURCE-CELL ring. The InMAP grid is a quadtree of axis-aligned rectangles, so four corners with implicit closure describe a cell exactly."
  },
  "N_XY": {
   "type": "integer",
   "default": 2,
   "description": "Components of a planar coordinate: easting at position 1, northing at position 2. The xy axis of every ring array, and of the reader's `geometry` (where the two are lon and lat, before projection)."
  },
  "N_REC": {
   "type": "integer",
   "default": 0,
   "description": "Emission polygons. DISCOVERED, not declared: Polygon_Emis.extent binds it to the number of rows the reader delivers (one per shapefile PART), so a reduced run's `select` truncation re-discovers a smaller value by itself. The default 0 is never used - the loader always supplies it."
  }
 },
 "index_sets": {
  "src_cells": {
   "kind": "interval",
   "size": "N_SRC"
  },
  "rcv_cells": {
   "kind": "interval",
   "size": "N_RCV"
  },
  "all_cells": {
   "kind": "interval",
   "size": "N_ALL"
  },
  "emis_records": {
   "kind": "interval",
   "size": "N_REC"
  },
  "poly_vertex": {
   "kind": "interval",
   "size": "N_PVERT"
  },
  "poly_segment": {
   "kind": "interval",
   "size": "N_PSEG"
  },
  "cell_vertex": {
   "kind": "interval",
   "size": "N_CVERT"
  },
  "xy": {
   "kind": "interval",
   "size": "N_XY"
  }
 },
 "data_sources": {
  "ISRM_Grid": {
   "kind": "static",
   "source": {
    "url_template": "s3://inmap-model/isrm_v1.2.2.zarr/"
   },
   "reference": {
    "url": "https://inmap.run/blog/2022/12/15/tutorial/",
    "notes": "InMAP ISRM v1.2.1 zarr store on the inmap-model S3 bucket - the 1-D per-cell arrays: the grid rectangles, population and mortality. An area source needs no meteorology, so unlike isrm_point.esm this document does not read the eight plume-rise fields."
   },
   "metadata": {
    "esio_format": "zarr",
    "tags": [
     "isrm",
     "inmap",
     "grid",
     "zarr",
     "health"
    ],
    "x_esd": {
     "format": "zarr",
     "zarr_format": 2,
     "consolidated": false,
     "arrays": {
      "TotalPop": {
       "shape": [
        596444
       ],
       "chunks": [
        596444
       ],
       "dtype": "<f8"
      },
      "MortalityRate": {
       "shape": [
        596444
       ],
       "chunks": [
        596444
       ],
       "dtype": "<f8"
      },
      "W": {
       "shape": [
        596444
       ],
       "chunks": [
        596444
       ],
       "dtype": "<f8"
      },
      "S": {
       "shape": [
        596444
       ],
       "chunks": [
        596444
       ],
       "dtype": "<f8"
      },
      "E": {
       "shape": [
        596444
       ],
       "chunks": [
        596444
       ],
       "dtype": "<f8"
      },
      "N": {
       "shape": [
        596444
       ],
       "chunks": [
        596444
       ],
       "dtype": "<f8"
      }
     },
     "cell_layout": "One value per cell of the FULL InMAP grid, laid out LAYER-MAJOR over the model's 27 layers: 8 ground-grid layers of 52411 cells each, then 19 coarse layers of 9324, and 8*52411 + 19*9324 = 596444 = N_ALL. The source and receptor sets are the leading N_SRC / N_RCV cells - model layer 0 - which is what the `select` ranges below take.",
     "note": "`format` and the pinned .zarray params are not expressible in the 0.8.0 source schema; the runner sets format at provider creation. Everything here is a single-chunk 1-D array read in full."
    }
   }
  },
  "ISRM_SR_L0": {
   "kind": "static",
   "source": {
    "url_template": "s3://inmap-model/isrm_v1.2.2.zarr/"
   },
   "reference": {
    "url": "https://inmap.run/blog/2022/12/15/tutorial/",
    "notes": "InMAP ISRM v1.2.1 zarr store, emission layer 0 - the only emission layer a ground-level area source reaches."
   },
   "metadata": {
    "esio_format": "zarr",
    "tags": [
     "isrm",
     "source_receptor",
     "air_quality",
     "zarr",
     "emis_layer_0"
    ],
    "x_esd": {
     "format": "zarr",
     "zarr_format": 2,
     "consolidated": false,
     "sr_dim_order": [
      "emisLayer",
      "source",
      "receptor"
     ],
     "arrays": {
      "PrimaryPM25": {
       "shape": [
        3,
        52411,
        52411
       ],
       "chunks": [
        1,
        5,
        52411
       ],
       "dtype": "<f4",
       "compressor": "blosc:lz4:clevel5:shuffle1",
       "fill": 0.0,
       "order": "C"
      }
     },
     "gated_select": {
      "axes": [
       {
        "fixed": [
         0
        ]
       },
       {
        "gated_by": "emis_src_cells"
       },
       "all"
      ],
      "applies_to": [
       "PrimaryPM25"
      ]
     },
     "note": "Per-axis selection over the native SR dims [emisLayer, source, receptor] = [3, 52411, 52411]. `{\"fixed\":[0]}` takes emission layer 0 and DROPS the axis; `{\"gated_by\":\"emis_src_cells\"}` takes the DERIVED support set the pushdown rewrite invents from this document's own spatial gate, in its canonical member order; `\"all\"` keeps the receptor axis. The support set here is every cell an emission POLYGON overlaps, not every cell that contains an emission POINT, and it is the rewrite that works that out - the difference between this document and isrm_point.esm never leaves the model. Only the one pathway the example layer feeds is declared; the sibling loaders ISRM_SR_L1/L2 of isrm_point.esm have no counterpart here because an area source emits at the ground."
    }
   }
  },
  "Emis": {
   "kind": "static",
   "source": {
    "url_template": "REPLACED-BY-THE-CALLER"
   },
   "reference": {
    "notes": "The caller's own polygon emissions, written out of a GeoDataFrame as a zipped shapefile set and uploaded as an EarthSciLab dataset. The url is a placeholder: a dispatched run reads the document's own loaders, so the caller rewrites this with the dataset's store url before it sends the document. There is no upstream copy to point at, because the layer is the caller's."
   },
   "metadata": {
    "esio_format": "shapefile",
    "tags": [
     "shapefile",
     "polygon",
     "emissions",
     "user_supplied"
    ],
    "x_esd": {
     "format": "shapefile",
     "container": "zip",
     "crs": "EPSG:4269",
     "note": "The reader is READER-ONLY: it decodes rings and .dbf columns and declares the file's CRS, and it reprojects nothing. `member` names the .shp inside the zip (the .dbf/.shx/.prj sidecars are the same stem) and never enters the cache key. `nvert_max` is the DECLARED vertex-axis width: every part is right-padded to it by repeating its final vertex, and the reader raises rather than silently widening, so the caller computes it from the frame it is about to write. One row per shapefile PART, with the record's .dbf attributes replicated.",
     "columns": {
      "PM25": "kg/yr",
      "VOC": "kg/yr",
      "NOx": "kg/yr",
      "NH3": "kg/yr",
      "SOx": "kg/yr"
     }
    }
   },
   "reader_options": {
    "member": "emis.shp",
    "nvert_max": 56
   },
   "extent": {
    "metaparameter": "N_REC"
   }
  }
 },
 "models": {
  "ISRM": {
   "system_kind": "nonlinear",
   "expression_template_imports": [
    {
     "ref": "./isrm_base.esm"
    }
   ],
   "reference": {
    "url": "https://inmap.run/blog/2019/04/20/sr/",
    "notes": "Same grid, same SR matrix, same unit conversion and same health function as isrm_point.esm; a different emission geometry."
   },
   "variables": {
    "W": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "m",
     "default": 0.0,
     "shape": [
      "all_cells"
     ],
     "description": "Cell west edge (LCC m); from ISRM_Grid.W (full N_ALL, the layer-0 prefix used).",
     "update": {
      "kind": "data",
      "source": "ISRM_Grid",
      "from": {
       "file_variable": "W"
      }
     }
    },
    "S": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "m",
     "default": 0.0,
     "shape": [
      "all_cells"
     ],
     "description": "Cell south edge (LCC m); from ISRM_Grid.S (full N_ALL, the layer-0 prefix used).",
     "update": {
      "kind": "data",
      "source": "ISRM_Grid",
      "from": {
       "file_variable": "S"
      }
     }
    },
    "E": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "m",
     "default": 0.0,
     "shape": [
      "all_cells"
     ],
     "description": "Cell east edge (LCC m); from ISRM_Grid.E (full N_ALL, the layer-0 prefix used).",
     "update": {
      "kind": "data",
      "source": "ISRM_Grid",
      "from": {
       "file_variable": "E"
      }
     }
    },
    "N": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "m",
     "default": 0.0,
     "shape": [
      "all_cells"
     ],
     "description": "Cell north edge (LCC m); from ISRM_Grid.N (full N_ALL, the layer-0 prefix used).",
     "update": {
      "kind": "data",
      "source": "ISRM_Grid",
      "from": {
       "file_variable": "N"
      }
     }
    },
    "TotalPop": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "1",
     "default": 0.0,
     "shape": [
      "all_cells"
     ],
     "description": "Population per cell; from ISRM_Grid.TotalPop (read on the rcv range = the [0:N_RCV] prefix slice).",
     "update": {
      "kind": "data",
      "source": "ISRM_Grid",
      "from": {
       "file_variable": "TotalPop"
      }
     }
    },
    "MortalityRate": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "1",
     "default": 0.0,
     "shape": [
      "all_cells"
     ],
     "description": "Baseline mortality per 100k; from ISRM_Grid.MortalityRate (prefix [0:N_RCV]).",
     "update": {
      "kind": "data",
      "source": "ISRM_Grid",
      "from": {
       "file_variable": "MortalityRate"
      }
     }
    },
    "src_W": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "m",
     "default": 0.0,
     "shape": [
      "src_cells"
     ],
     "description": "Source-cell rectangle W-edge (projected LCC metres), src_cells axis: the declared [0:N_SRC] prefix of the full-grid W. These four are the cell side of the broad-phase gate AND the corners `rect_ring_xy` builds cell_ring from.",
     "update": {
      "kind": "data",
      "source": "ISRM_Grid",
      "from": {
       "file_variable": "W",
       "select": {
        "axes": [
         {
          "range": {
           "start": 0,
           "stop": "N_SRC"
          }
         }
        ]
       }
      }
     }
    },
    "src_S": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "m",
     "default": 0.0,
     "shape": [
      "src_cells"
     ],
     "description": "Source-cell rectangle S-edge (projected LCC metres), src_cells axis: the declared [0:N_SRC] prefix of the full-grid S. These four are the cell side of the broad-phase gate AND the corners `rect_ring_xy` builds cell_ring from.",
     "update": {
      "kind": "data",
      "source": "ISRM_Grid",
      "from": {
       "file_variable": "S",
       "select": {
        "axes": [
         {
          "range": {
           "start": 0,
           "stop": "N_SRC"
          }
         }
        ]
       }
      }
     }
    },
    "src_E": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "m",
     "default": 0.0,
     "shape": [
      "src_cells"
     ],
     "description": "Source-cell rectangle E-edge (projected LCC metres), src_cells axis: the declared [0:N_SRC] prefix of the full-grid E. These four are the cell side of the broad-phase gate AND the corners `rect_ring_xy` builds cell_ring from.",
     "update": {
      "kind": "data",
      "source": "ISRM_Grid",
      "from": {
       "file_variable": "E",
       "select": {
        "axes": [
         {
          "range": {
           "start": 0,
           "stop": "N_SRC"
          }
         }
        ]
       }
      }
     }
    },
    "src_N": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "m",
     "default": 0.0,
     "shape": [
      "src_cells"
     ],
     "description": "Source-cell rectangle N-edge (projected LCC metres), src_cells axis: the declared [0:N_SRC] prefix of the full-grid N. These four are the cell side of the broad-phase gate AND the corners `rect_ring_xy` builds cell_ring from.",
     "update": {
      "kind": "data",
      "source": "ISRM_Grid",
      "from": {
       "file_variable": "N",
       "select": {
        "axes": [
         {
          "range": {
           "start": 0,
           "stop": "N_SRC"
          }
         }
        ]
       }
      }
     }
    },
    "poly_lonlat": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "degree",
     "default": 0.0,
     "shape": [
      "emis_records",
      "poly_vertex",
      "xy"
     ],
     "description": "Every emission polygon's ring, as the shapefile stores it: [record, vertex, component] in the file's own CRS (EPSG:4269 lon/lat), right-padded to N_PVERT vertices by repeating each ring's final vertex. Component 1 is longitude and component 2 is latitude. From Polygon_Emis.geometry. This is the ONLY geometry input - `n_vertices` is deliberately not read, because repeat-padding is already the ring's deduplicated form (esm-spec 8.6.1) and a real vertex count would only be a second way to say the same thing.",
     "update": {
      "kind": "data",
      "source": "Emis",
      "from": {
       "file_variable": "geometry"
      }
     }
    },
    "fact": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "1",
     "default": 31.709791983764585,
     "description": "kg/yr -> ug/s: 1e9 ug/kg over 3.1536e7 s/yr. The published documents' 28766.639396245562 is this times 907.18474 kg per short ton, so reading SI changes this constant and nothing else."
    },
    "pop_scale": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "1",
     "default": 1.0465819687408728,
     "description": "Population 2010->2016 rescale."
    },
    "mort_scale": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "1",
     "default": 1.025229357798165,
     "description": "Mortality-rate 2005->2016 rescale."
    },
    "rr_K": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "1",
     "default": 1.06,
     "description": "Krewski relative risk per 10 ug/m3 PM2.5."
    },
    "rr_L": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "1",
     "default": 1.14,
     "description": "Lepeule relative risk per 10 ug/m3 PM2.5."
    },
    "lat_1": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "degree",
     "default": 33.0,
     "description": "LCC standard parallel 1 (NEI2016)."
    },
    "lat_2": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "degree",
     "default": 45.0,
     "description": "LCC standard parallel 2 (NEI2016)."
    },
    "lat_0": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "degree",
     "default": 40.0,
     "description": "LCC origin latitude (NEI2016)."
    },
    "lon_0": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "degree",
     "default": -97.0,
     "description": "LCC central meridian (NEI2016)."
    },
    "lcc_R": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "m",
     "default": 6370997.0,
     "description": "LCC sphere radius (NEI2016). (Named lcc_R, not R, to avoid the reserved physical-constant name R = ideal gas constant.)"
    },
    "lcc_d2r": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "default": 0.017453292519943295,
     "description": "degrees->radians (pi/180) for the LCC projection."
    },
    "lcc_qp": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "default": 0.7853981633974483,
     "description": "pi/4 quarter-turn offset for the LCC tan half-angle."
    },
    "poly_x": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m",
     "shape": [
      "emis_records",
      "poly_vertex"
     ],
     "description": "Lambert-conformal-conic forward-x of every polygon vertex, in the InMAP grid's own projected metres - the same lcc_forward_x template isrm_point.esm projects its emission points with. A pure MAP over (record, vertex): the padded repeats project to repeats, so the padding survives projection as padding."
    },
    "poly_y": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m",
     "shape": [
      "emis_records",
      "poly_vertex"
     ],
     "description": "Lambert-conformal-conic forward-y of every polygon vertex. See poly_x."
    },
    "poly_ring": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m",
     "shape": [
      "emis_records",
      "poly_vertex",
      "xy"
     ],
     "description": "Every emission polygon's ring in PROJECTED metres: poly_x and poly_y woven back into [record, vertex, xy]. This is the operand `polygon_intersection_area` clips against the cell rings, and it is in the same planar coordinate system they are, which is what makes `manifold: planar` the right answer rather than a convenient one."
    },
    "poly_xmin": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m",
     "shape": [
      "emis_records"
     ],
     "description": "The western extreme of the PROJECTED polygon - the record side of the broad-phase gate. Taken over the projected vertices rather than from the shapefile's own stored bounding box because the Lambert image of a lon/lat rectangle is not a rectangle: its western extreme need not be at a corner, so projecting the stored box would not be conservative and a gate that is not conservative drops real overlaps silently. The padding repeats a vertex the ring already has, so it cannot move an extreme."
    },
    "poly_xmax": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m",
     "shape": [
      "emis_records"
     ],
     "description": "The eastern extreme of the PROJECTED polygon - the record side of the broad-phase gate. Taken over the projected vertices rather than from the shapefile's own stored bounding box because the Lambert image of a lon/lat rectangle is not a rectangle: its eastern extreme need not be at a corner, so projecting the stored box would not be conservative and a gate that is not conservative drops real overlaps silently. The padding repeats a vertex the ring already has, so it cannot move an extreme."
    },
    "poly_ymin": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m",
     "shape": [
      "emis_records"
     ],
     "description": "The southern extreme of the PROJECTED polygon - the record side of the broad-phase gate. Taken over the projected vertices rather than from the shapefile's own stored bounding box because the Lambert image of a lon/lat rectangle is not a rectangle: its southern extreme need not be at a corner, so projecting the stored box would not be conservative and a gate that is not conservative drops real overlaps silently. The padding repeats a vertex the ring already has, so it cannot move an extreme."
    },
    "poly_ymax": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m",
     "shape": [
      "emis_records"
     ],
     "description": "The northern extreme of the PROJECTED polygon - the record side of the broad-phase gate. Taken over the projected vertices rather than from the shapefile's own stored bounding box because the Lambert image of a lon/lat rectangle is not a rectangle: its northern extreme need not be at a corner, so projecting the stored box would not be conservative and a gate that is not conservative drops real overlaps silently. The padding repeats a vertex the ring already has, so it cannot move an extreme."
    },
    "poly_cross2": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m^2",
     "shape": [
      "emis_records"
     ],
     "description": "Twice each polygon's SIGNED planar area: the shoelace sum over its ring segments. The sign is the ring's winding, which for a shapefile outer ring is clockwise and so negative in the projected right-handed frame; poly_area takes the magnitude. Split from poly_area because the sum is a reduction and the absolute value is not - a reduction cannot wrap its own result."
    },
    "poly_area": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m^2",
     "shape": [
      "emis_records"
     ],
     "description": "Each polygon's own area in projected metres squared - the denominator that makes the per-cell shares sum to 1. Three things it deliberately is NOT. It is not the shapefile's ALAND column: ALAND is geodesic LAND area, excluding water, while this is the planar area of the very ring the numerator clips, and using the column instead would leave a several-percent mass leak that no test would name. It is not a geometry-kernel result: `polygon_intersection_area(ring, ring)` is the obvious spelling and it is silently wrong for a non-convex ring, which most real polygons are (see the `ring_cross_term` and `area_share` template notes). And it is not the sum of this polygon's own cell overlaps, which would force the shares to 1 by construction and hide a polygon hanging off the edge of the domain - a polygon that half leaves the grid SHOULD deliver half its mass."
    },
    "cell_ring": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m",
     "shape": [
      "src_cells",
      "cell_vertex",
      "xy"
     ],
     "description": "Every source cell's rectangle as a four-vertex ring in projected metres, built from src_W/src_S/src_E/src_N by `rect_ring_xy`. The InMAP grid ships as four edge arrays, not as rings, so a document that wants to clip against cells has to make them. Its declared leading axis is src_cells, which is what makes the projection-pushdown rewrite gather it onto the derived support set alongside the envelope bounds - rank-preservingly, so `cell_ring[c]` stays a [cell_vertex, xy] ring after the rewrite (CONFORMANCE_SPEC 5.5.7)."
    },
    "TotalPM25": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "ug/m^3",
     "shape": [
      "rcv_cells"
     ],
     "description": "Total PM2.5 concentration at each receptor: fact times the sum of every pathway this document wires (PrimaryPM25, SOA, pNO3, pNH4, pSO4). This is the quantity the health functions consume."
    },
    "deathsK": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1",
     "shape": [
      "rcv_cells"
     ],
     "description": "Attributable deaths at each receptor (Krewski RR 1.06): (exp(log(rr)/10*TotalPM25)-1)*TotalPop*pop_scale*MortalityRate/100000*mort_scale. TotalPop/MortalityRate indexed on the rcv range = the [0:N_RCV] prefix slice."
    },
    "deathsL": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "1",
     "shape": [
      "rcv_cells"
     ],
     "description": "Attributable deaths at each receptor (Lepeule RR 1.14): (exp(log(rr)/10*TotalPM25)-1)*TotalPop*pop_scale*MortalityRate/100000*mort_scale. TotalPop/MortalityRate indexed on the rcv range = the [0:N_RCV] prefix slice."
    },
    "rcv_W": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m",
     "shape": [
      "rcv_cells"
     ],
     "description": "Receptor cell rectangle west edge in LCC metres: the [0:N_RCV] prefix of the full-grid W. Written so a run's output store carries the geometry its fields are over."
    },
    "rcv_S": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m",
     "shape": [
      "rcv_cells"
     ],
     "description": "Receptor cell rectangle south edge in LCC metres: the [0:N_RCV] prefix of the full-grid S. Written so a run's output store carries the geometry its fields are over."
    },
    "rcv_E": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m",
     "shape": [
      "rcv_cells"
     ],
     "description": "Receptor cell rectangle east edge in LCC metres: the [0:N_RCV] prefix of the full-grid E. Written so a run's output store carries the geometry its fields are over."
    },
    "rcv_N": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m",
     "shape": [
      "rcv_cells"
     ],
     "description": "Receptor cell rectangle north edge in LCC metres: the [0:N_RCV] prefix of the full-grid N. Written so a run's output store carries the geometry its fields are over."
    },
    "rcv_cx": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m",
     "shape": [
      "rcv_cells"
     ],
     "description": "Receptor cell-centroid easting in LCC metres."
    },
    "rcv_cy": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "m",
     "shape": [
      "rcv_cells"
     ],
     "description": "Receptor cell-centroid northing in LCC metres."
    },
    "emis_PM25": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "kg/yr",
     "default": 0.0,
     "shape": [
      "emis_records"
     ],
     "description": "Each polygon's annual PM25 emission in kilograms per year, from Emis.PM25. It feeds the PrimaryPM25 pathway. Absent from the caller's frame this column is not written and the pathway is pruned from the document, so a variable that survives here always has a column behind it. `fact` is the kg/yr -> ug/s conversion, applied once to the summed concentration.",
     "update": {
      "kind": "data",
      "source": "Emis",
      "from": {
       "file_variable": "PM25"
      }
     }
    },
    "emis_VOC": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "kg/yr",
     "default": 0.0,
     "shape": [
      "emis_records"
     ],
     "description": "Each polygon's annual VOC emission in kilograms per year, from Emis.VOC. It feeds the SOA pathway. Absent from the caller's frame this column is not written and the pathway is pruned from the document, so a variable that survives here always has a column behind it. `fact` is the kg/yr -> ug/s conversion, applied once to the summed concentration.",
     "update": {
      "kind": "data",
      "source": "Emis",
      "from": {
       "file_variable": "VOC"
      }
     }
    },
    "emis_NOx": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "kg/yr",
     "default": 0.0,
     "shape": [
      "emis_records"
     ],
     "description": "Each polygon's annual NOx emission in kilograms per year, from Emis.NOx. It feeds the pNO3 pathway. Absent from the caller's frame this column is not written and the pathway is pruned from the document, so a variable that survives here always has a column behind it. `fact` is the kg/yr -> ug/s conversion, applied once to the summed concentration.",
     "update": {
      "kind": "data",
      "source": "Emis",
      "from": {
       "file_variable": "NOx"
      }
     }
    },
    "emis_NH3": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "kg/yr",
     "default": 0.0,
     "shape": [
      "emis_records"
     ],
     "description": "Each polygon's annual NH3 emission in kilograms per year, from Emis.NH3. It feeds the pNH4 pathway. Absent from the caller's frame this column is not written and the pathway is pruned from the document, so a variable that survives here always has a column behind it. `fact` is the kg/yr -> ug/s conversion, applied once to the summed concentration.",
     "update": {
      "kind": "data",
      "source": "Emis",
      "from": {
       "file_variable": "NH3"
      }
     }
    },
    "emis_SOx": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "kg/yr",
     "default": 0.0,
     "shape": [
      "emis_records"
     ],
     "description": "Each polygon's annual SOx emission in kilograms per year, from Emis.SOx. It feeds the pSO4 pathway. Absent from the caller's frame this column is not written and the pathway is pruned from the document, so a variable that survives here always has a column behind it. `fact` is the kg/yr -> ug/s conversion, applied once to the summed concentration.",
     "update": {
      "kind": "data",
      "source": "Emis",
      "from": {
       "file_variable": "SOx"
      }
     }
    },
    "E_PM25": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "kg/yr",
     "shape": [
      "src_cells"
     ],
     "description": "PM25 emissions binned into each source cell: the sum over records of the record's PM25 times the fraction of the record that lies in the cell. The gate ifelse MUST stay the FIRST ifelse in this body — the projection-pushdown rewrite reads it to derive the support set and the SR gate, and a body it cannot recognise does not fail, it silently drops out of `applies_to` and the whole SR slab is fetched ungated."
    },
    "E_VOC": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "kg/yr",
     "shape": [
      "src_cells"
     ],
     "description": "VOC emissions binned into each source cell: the sum over records of the record's VOC times the fraction of the record that lies in the cell. The gate ifelse MUST stay the FIRST ifelse in this body — the projection-pushdown rewrite reads it to derive the support set and the SR gate, and a body it cannot recognise does not fail, it silently drops out of `applies_to` and the whole SR slab is fetched ungated."
    },
    "E_NOx": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "kg/yr",
     "shape": [
      "src_cells"
     ],
     "description": "NOx emissions binned into each source cell: the sum over records of the record's NOx times the fraction of the record that lies in the cell. The gate ifelse MUST stay the FIRST ifelse in this body — the projection-pushdown rewrite reads it to derive the support set and the SR gate, and a body it cannot recognise does not fail, it silently drops out of `applies_to` and the whole SR slab is fetched ungated."
    },
    "E_NH3": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "kg/yr",
     "shape": [
      "src_cells"
     ],
     "description": "NH3 emissions binned into each source cell: the sum over records of the record's NH3 times the fraction of the record that lies in the cell. The gate ifelse MUST stay the FIRST ifelse in this body — the projection-pushdown rewrite reads it to derive the support set and the SR gate, and a body it cannot recognise does not fail, it silently drops out of `applies_to` and the whole SR slab is fetched ungated."
    },
    "E_SOx": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "kg/yr",
     "shape": [
      "src_cells"
     ],
     "description": "SOx emissions binned into each source cell: the sum over records of the record's SOx times the fraction of the record that lies in the cell. The gate ifelse MUST stay the FIRST ifelse in this body — the projection-pushdown rewrite reads it to derive the support set and the SR gate, and a body it cannot recognise does not fail, it silently drops out of `applies_to` and the whole SR slab is fetched ungated."
    },
    "SR_PrimaryPM25_L0": {
     "type": "parameter",
     "units": "ug/m^3/(kg/yr)",
     "default": 0.0,
     "shape": [
      "src_cells",
      "rcv_cells"
     ],
     "description": "Source-receptor matrix for the PrimaryPM25 pathway from emission layer 0. Rows are the emission-bearing source cells the projection-pushdown rewrite derives, columns the N_RCV receptors. Wired from ISRM_SR_L0.PrimaryPM25; the loader's own gated_select fixes the emission-layer axis, so this stays rank-2.",
     "update": {
      "kind": "data",
      "source": "ISRM_SR_L0",
      "from": {
       "file_variable": "PrimaryPM25"
      }
     }
    },
    "conc_PrimaryPM25": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "ug/m^3",
     "shape": [
      "rcv_cells"
     ],
     "description": "Per-receptor concentration response from the PrimaryPM25 pathway: the layer-0 SR matrix contracted with the per-cell emissions. One contraction rather than the point document's three, because an area or line source reaches only emission layer 0."
    },
    "pm_PrimaryPM25": {
     "type": "unknown",
     "units": "ug/m^3",
     "shape": [
      "rcv_cells"
     ],
     "description": "Reported PrimaryPM25 PM2.5 component at each receptor: fact*conc_PrimaryPM25."
    },
    "SR_SOA_L0": {
